@@ -0,0 +1,230 @@
+# Project archives, version 1
+
+This optional storage mode groups project files into independently readable ZIP
+blocks. It is part of the normal backup, validation and transactional restoration
+paths. It does not change the storage of conversations, attachments or the
+portable Codex profile. Ordinary file backups remain the default.
+
+## Enabling the mode
+
+Select the project archive option in the backup screen, or set this value in the
+backup configuration:
+
+```json
+{
+  "projectStorage": "archive-blocks-v1"
+}
+```
+
+The backup module also accepts `--project-storage archive-blocks-v1`; explicitly
+passing `--project-storage files` selects the original layout. The Python backup
+entry point reads `args.project_storage` first, then `projectStorage` from the
+configuration, then defaults to `files`. Other values are rejected.
+
+Archive backups declare package `formatVersion: "2.5"` and
+`projectStorage: "archive-blocks-v1"`. Existing file backups still declare version
+2.4. This reader accepts the previous 2.0–2.4 versions. An older reader that does
+not support 2.5 must reject the new package rather than mistake absent project
+directories for empty projects. Changing a manifest version does not convert a
+backup. Recreate a backup in the desired mode.
+
+The archive option is applied to every included project in a 2.5 package. It does
+not convert or delete existing backups, and no automatic change of storage mode
+is made during restoration.
+
+## On-disk contract
+
+`manifest/projects.json` retains each project's logical `backupRelativePath`,
+`fileCount` and `totalBytes`. An archived project adds this descriptor:
+
+```json
+{
+  "backupRelativePath": "projects/root-example",
+  "fileCount": 1,
+  "totalBytes": 5,
+  "storage": {
+    "kind": "archive-blocks-v1",
+    "metadata": "mode-mtime-ns-v1",
+    "blocks": ["project-archives/<project-key>/block-000000.zip"]
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`<project-key>` is the first 24 lowercase hexadecimal characters of SHA-256 of
+the UTF-8 logical `backupRelativePath`. Block names are consecutive, six-digit
+indexes starting at zero, in the exact order recorded in `blocks`. Each project
+has at least one block, including a project containing only empty directories.
+There is no simultaneous physical `projects/root-example` payload tree.
+
+A block contains `lifeboat-index.json` plus generated file objects. ZIP names do
+not contain user paths. A representative index is:
+
+```json
+{
+  "archiveVersion": 1,
+  "entries": [
+    {"path": "", "kind": "directory", "mode": 493, "mtimeNs": 1700000000000000000},
+    {"path": "hello.txt", "kind": "file", "mode": 420, "mtimeNs": 1700000000000000000,
+     "size": 5,
+     "sha256": "2cf24dba5fb0a30e26e83b2ac5b9e29e1b161e5c1fa7425e73043362938b9824",
+     "member": "files/00000001"}
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+The example file object contains the five UTF-8 bytes `hello`. `member` identifies
+the zero-based position of that entry within its block, padded to eight digits.
+Directories have no ZIP payload member. The project root is the unique directory
+entry with an empty path; every parent directory precedes its descendants across
+the ordered blocks. Empty files and empty directories are explicit entries.
+Unknown entry metadata, duplicate JSON keys, duplicate paths and case collisions
+are rejected. This version does not accept links or implicit directories.
+
+Writer and reader limits are:
+
+| Property | Limit or behavior |
+| --- | --- |
+| Entries per block | At most 1,024, counting files and directories |
+| Uncompressed file data per block | Target/max 128 MiB; a single larger file is allowed in its own block |
+| Index size | At most 4 MiB uncompressed |
+| File I/O buffer | At most 1 MiB per streaming read |
+| ZIP central directory | At most 1,025 entries and 262,400 bytes, checked before `ZipFile` allocates its inventory |
+| ZIP representation | Stored index, DEFLATE file data at level 1; ZIP64 for large files/offsets |
+| Accepted compression on read | Stored or DEFLATE only |
+| ZIP extras | No encryption, split volumes, archive comments, trailing data or undeclared members |
+| Project path inventory | Temporary SQLite registry, with a 2 MiB page cache, for cross-block collision detection |
+
+The implementation never invokes `extractall` or trusts ZIP filenames as output
+paths. Each output path is derived from the validated index. Canonical paths use
+`/` and cannot be absolute, contain `..`, empty components, alternate stream
+syntax, Windows reserved device names, forbidden characters, trailing spaces or
+periods, or the internal `.lifeboat-archive-` temporary-file prefix. Component
+and total path lengths are checked against portable Windows name limits. Case
+collisions are rejected on every platform. Existing links, reparse points and
+nonregular nodes in the extraction tree are rejected.
+
+## Integrity and metadata
+
+`manifest/sha256.csv` contains both physical archive block rows and the original
+logical `projects/root-example/hello.txt` file rows. The logical rows preserve
+project fingerprints and lineage comparison. Package `hashedFiles` counts both
+sets; `payloadBytes` counts physical stored bytes only. The validator reconciles
+the two inventories and verifies member SHA-256 while streaming decompression,
+without creating an intermediate project tree. Physical block SHA-256 includes
+the directory and file metadata indexes as well as file bytes.
+
+Normal restoration verifies the physical blocks against the manifest, bounds
+each decompression by its declared size, verifies each file SHA-256, and then
+performs the existing staged and final project hash checks. Metadata checks are
+also bound to the manifest's block hashes. A valid ZIP CRC alone does not satisfy
+integrity verification. These checks detect corruption relative to the supplied
+manifest; the package format is not a signed provenance format.
+
+The `mode-mtime-ns-v1` contract is deliberately specific:
+
+| Property | Contract |
+| --- | --- |
+| File contents | Exact bytes, including empty files, verified by SHA-256 |
+| Directory structure | Explicit root, nested directories and empty directories; final set checked |
+| Modification time | Exact `st_mtime_ns` for files and directories; verified after writes |
+| POSIX mode | The ordinary `0o777` permission bits |
+| Windows mode | Read-only/writable bits exposed by Python; not POSIX executable semantics |
+| Last access time | Not promised: required integrity reads can update it |
+| Creation time, owner, Windows ACLs, other Windows attributes | Not preserved by this mode; normal destination inheritance applies |
+| Alternate data streams and EFS | Rejected before archiving; no silent loss of stream contents or EFS state |
+| Extended attributes, including POSIX ACL xattrs | Rejected where the platform exposes them |
+| Symlinks, junctions/reparse points, hardlinked regular files, devices and other special files | Rejected |
+| Setuid/setgid/sticky permission bits | Rejected |
+
+This is not a disk image or a complete copy of NTFS metadata. Windows stream
+enumeration is mandatory: if it cannot establish that a source has no unsupported
+streams, archiving fails with a readable reason. Source files are checked before
+and after reading so a changing file is not silently presented as a coherent
+copy. Configured exclusions and the existing explicitly reported reconstructable
+cache exception still apply. An unsupported source tree can be backed up using
+the original file mode, whose own supported-metadata behavior is unchanged.
+
+Exact timestamp and mode verification requires a destination filesystem that can
+represent the source values. For example, NTFS timestamps have 100 ns granularity;
+an arbitrary nanosecond timestamp from another filesystem may not be representable.
+A mismatch fails verification before an unverified staged project is committed.
+No approximate timestamp comparison is substituted. Source or destination trees
+must not be changed concurrently during a backup or restore.
+
+## Restoration, disk space and resume
+
+The archive is written directly from its source during backup; there is no second
+fully expanded project payload in the backup. The existing backup preflight space
+estimate remains conservative, based on source bytes. While writing blocks,
+available space is checked at least once per second of streaming I/O, with a
+100 MiB reserve in addition to the next buffer. Actual I/O failures still abort
+the backup. Backup creation itself does not acquire a new pause/resume protocol.
+
+Restoration extracts directly into the existing private project staging tree.
+It retains the same destination, rollback and commit rules as file-mode restore.
+Archive compression reduces backup storage when data compresses; the destination
+still needs enough room for expanded files, the current destination where kept
+for rollback, temporary files, and the existing restore reserve. The normal
+restore space probe runs before writes and is repeated during a large file.
+
+For each file the extractor writes a reserved temporary sibling, verifies data,
+flushes and fsyncs it, and closes the content-writing handle. It then applies
+metadata and atomically replaces its staged target. The timestamp is applied
+after closing the writing handle so Windows cannot replace it on that close.
+Only then is the file entered in the existing durable copy checkpoint.
+If a file is interrupted, its temporary output is removed when possible; on
+resume it is recreated and checked. Recorded completed files are not decompressed
+again. They remain subject to final content and metadata verification. A completed
+project is skipped by the normal durable restore operation when a later phase is
+resumed.
+
+Pause checks run between entries, during block hashing, during streamed file
+decompression and while applying directory metadata. Directory modes and mtimes
+are applied after their descendants, deepest first. A graceful pause flushes the
+checkpoint. An abrupt termination can replay the uncommitted checkpoint batch;
+it does not turn a partial file into verified progress. If saving a checkpoint
+also fails during another error, the original error is retained and uncommitted
+files are safely replayed. Blocking filesystem calls cannot be interrupted in the
+middle by the cooperative pause mechanism.
+
+Rollback and managed recovery-point retention can remove restored read-only
+files. The existing path-authorization checks still select the deletion scope.
+Only a Windows access-denied error on an ordinary node with the read-only
+attribute triggers a retry after clearing that attribute; reparse points and
+other permission/sharing failures are not treated as read-only exceptions.
+
+When the destination has the same file content but differs in archived timestamps,
+modes or empty directories, comparison requests the normal explicit source/target
+decision. It does not silently overwrite those differences. Source Git-worktree
+inspection is unavailable until an archived project has been materialized;
+the comparison reports that limitation and keeps content/lineage checks.
+
+## Acceptance checks and performance status
+
+`benchmarks/test_archive_optimization.py` covers multi-block round trips, exact
+metadata, empty projects, logical inventory, manifest indexes, pause/reopen,
+space failures, corrupted blocks and file objects, unsafe paths, special files,
+duplicate names, oversized ZIP inventories and unsupported metadata. The Windows
+cases use the real backup/restore pipeline, exercise rollback on a staging
+metadata fault, and prove a project completed before pause is not extracted a
+second time. Windows also checks rejection of an NTFS alternate data stream.
+The rollback fixture contains a read-only archived file, and a separate Windows
+retention case removes read-only data only from the expired approved point while
+keeping the two retained points and the active destination.
+
+Run the portable and platform-specific tests with:
+
+```text
+python -m unittest discover -s benchmarks -p test_archive_optimization.py -v
+```
+
+The release build's packaged check is `--archive-self-test --work <empty-work-dir>`.
+It runs backup, validation, pause, resume and final verification from the packaged
+executable with archive storage enabled. Source tests on another operating system
+do not replace this Windows gate.
+
+Grouping files is an opt-in storage strategy, not a measured throughput promise.
+The version performs compression, block hashing, per-file hashing and final
+verification; CPU cost and repeated reads can outweigh fewer directory operations
+on some datasets. Compare complete runs on the intended Windows machine, including
+these integrity checks, before claiming a gain or changing the default.
