@@ -0,0 +1,87 @@
+# Local conversation pre-reading
+
+This optional action reads files for up to 50 recent local conversations so the
+operating system can reuse recently read file pages. It does not start Codex or an
+agent. No message is sent, no tool or MCP server is started, and no conversation
+or original index is written.
+
+## What this feature establishes
+
+- Recent conversations are selected by `updated_at` from a bounded temporary
+  snapshot of `.codex/state_5.sqlite` and its WAL, excluding archived rows.
+- The original database is never opened by SQLite. A read-only SQLite connection
+  can still create WAL/SHM files or update SHM read marks; the snapshot keeps that
+  activity in a private temporary directory. The original SHM and credentials are
+  not copied. The temporary directory is deleted before file pre-reading.
+- Source sizes, modification times and file identities are checked around the
+  snapshot. This is not a guaranteed atomic view of a running Codex application.
+- If the index is absent, unsupported, changing or over budget, selection falls
+  back to file modification times only below `.codex/sessions`. The result reports
+  that fallback; file recency is not guaranteed to equal conversation recency.
+- Only ordinary files inside the selected Codex home are eligible. Parent
+  traversal, old-profile paths, symbolic links, junctions/reparse points,
+  hard-linked files, UNC roots and mapped network drives are rejected.
+- Plain JSONL and compressed JSONL are read as raw bytes. No parsing,
+  decompression, database checkpoint or history projection is performed.
+- Default limits are 50 files, 256 MiB of conversation data, 16 MiB per file,
+  64 MiB of metadata, 20,000 discovery entries and 60 seconds. The read budget is
+  shared among selected files. A partial read covers the beginning and end.
+- Cancellation is checked between chunks. A currently blocking OS read must
+  finish before cancellation can be observed.
+- Reports and progress contain only aggregate counts, durations and fixed reason
+  codes. They contain no titles, thread IDs, conversation text, credentials or
+  filesystem paths. The module makes no network calls and launches no process.
+
+## What it does not establish
+
+The result reports `mode: os_file_cache`, `applicationPreloadSupported: false`,
+`applicationCacheLoaded: false`, `persistentCache: false`, and
+`speedupMeasured: false`. `filesRead` counts files from which bytes were read (and
+empty files successfully opened); `partialFiles` counts incomplete pre-reads.
+These numbers do not mean Codex loaded a conversation or retained cache pages.
+
+The operating system controls eviction. Any benefit is temporary, depends on the
+machine and workload, and is not guaranteed after memory pressure or reboot. This
+does not avoid Codex's subsequent decompression, parsing, rendering, migration or
+model-context preparation. No application opening-time improvement is claimed
+without a measurement on the user's actual Codex application and profile.
+
+## Why no app-server warmup is invoked
+
+The official app-server documentation says `thread/read` reads stored metadata
+without resuming or subscribing, and explicitly says it does not load the thread
+into memory. `thread/turns/list` and `thread/items/list` can read history without
+resuming it. Full-history `includeTurns: true` is deprecated for paginated threads.
+`thread/resume` reopens a live thread and may initialize configured MCP servers.
+None of these contracts promises that a separate process can populate the Windows
+desktop application's persistent cache.
+
+The inspected OpenAI implementation also notes that legacy history pagination
+replays the rollout on each request; paginated histories use the thread store.
+That does not establish a supported cross-process desktop preloading contract.
+This feature therefore does not call app-server, `thread/resume`, `turn/start`,
+read-state updates, or internal history-store writes.
+
+Official references, inspected 8 November 2026:
+
+- [App-server: read a stored thread, list turns/items and resume](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/app-server)
+- [OpenAI Codex thread processor source](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/app-server/src/request_processors/thread_processor.rs)
+- [Official ThreadReadParams schema](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/app-server-protocol/schema/json/v2/ThreadReadParams.json)
+- [Official ThreadListParams schema](https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/app-server-protocol/schema/json/v2/ThreadListParams.json)
+
+## Integration
+
+```python
+from codex_transfer.conversation_warmup import warm_recent_conversations
+
+result = warm_recent_conversations(
+    actual_codex_home,
+    limit=50,
+    cancel=cancel_event,
+    progress=lambda aggregate: ui_queue.put(aggregate),
+)
+```
+
+Call it only after an explicit user action, on a worker thread. Do not use this
+operation while restoration is still changing the profile. The result is a local
+diagnostic summary, not a recovery plan or a record of application-loaded threads.
